**Not from `MessageInitShape` alone.** Generated `AuthConfig` / init still look like:

```ts
auth?: { credentials?: { case: "apiKey"; value: ... } | ... }
```

So this:

```ts
Omit<MessageInitShape<typeof ConfigSchema>, "auth"> & { auth?: ??? }
```

has nothing nested like `{ apiKey?: … }` to pull “directly” from the schema.

**You can drop the hand-written `AuthInput` object** by deriving it from the oneof:

```ts
type FlattenOneof<T extends { case: string | undefined; value?: unknown }> = {
  [K in Exclude<T["case"], undefined>]?: Omit<
    Extract<T, { case: K }>["value"] & object,
    "$typeName"
  >;
};

type AuthInput = FlattenOneof<AuthConfig["credentials"]>;
// → { apiKey?: …; tokenPair?: …; bearerToken?: …; oauthRefresh?: … }
```

Then:

```ts
export type ConfigInput = Omit<
  MessageInitShape<typeof ConfigSchema>,
  "auth"
> & { auth?: FlattenOneof<AuthConfig["credentials"]> };
```

Same public shape, no manual field list. You still need `materializeClientConfig` at runtime — types can’t rewrite `{ apiKey }` into `{ credentials: { case, value } }`.

**Summary:** protobuf won’t give nested auth on `ConfigInput` for free; a oneof→flat mapped type can replace writing `AuthInput` by hand. Switch to Agent mode if you want that applied in `client.ts`.

